# jailmgr (jailctl helper)

`jailmgr` provisions and runs multiple `jailctl` jails from one host-side configuration.
It uses one shared read-only base layer (from `base.tar.xz`) and one per-jail writable data tree mounted at `/.data`.
The base layer exposes writable paths via symlinks (`/etc`, `/var`, `/tmp`, `/home`, `/root`, `/usr/pkg` -> `/.data/...`).
It can also provide optional jail-local `/dev` (tmpfs + `MAKEDEV`).

## Important command semantics

- `create <name>` creates/updates the jail config and filesystem layout.
- `create -a <name>` does the same and initializes `JAIL_AUTOSTART=YES`.
- `create -s '<cmd ...>' <name>` initializes `JAIL_SUPERVISE_CMD` in `jail.conf`.
- `create -c <cpu-percent>` sets CPU limits for `jailctl create` (0-100; internally 1% = 10ms/s).
- `create -m <memory-mb>` sets memory limits for `jailctl create` (internally converted to bytes).
- `create -r <port[,port...]>` reserves one or more listening ports for the jail (`jailctl create -r`).
- `create -p <pri> -t <tag>` initializes logging options for `jailctl supervise`.
- `start <name>` and `restart <name>` use per-jail settings from `${JAIL_DATA_DIR}/<name>/jail.conf`.
- `start --all` / `stop --all` / `restart --all` work only on jails with `JAIL_AUTOSTART=YES`.

## Workflow

1. Install the helper script:

   ```sh
   install -m 0755 jailmgr /usr/sbin/jailmgr
   ```

2. Create `/etc/jailmgr.conf` from the sample and customize it.

3. Bootstrap shared release content:

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr bootstrap
   ```

   During bootstrap, `jailmgr` ensures:

   - module `secmodel_jail` is loaded (`modload secmodel_jail` when needed),
   - `/etc/modules.conf` exists,
   - `/etc/modules.conf` contains a `secmodel_jail` entry for reboot persistence.
   - `/etc/rc.conf` contains `jailmgr=` (initialized to `NO` if absent).

4. Create each jail (writes `${JAIL_DATA_DIR}/<name>/jail.conf`):

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr create jail1
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr create -a jail2
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr create -a -s '/bin/sh /etc/rc' \
       -c 25 -m 512 -r 80,443 -p local3.info -t jail-web jail3
   ```

   Then set at least `JAIL_SUPERVISE_CMD` and `JAIL_AUTOSTART` in each
   jail-local `jail.conf`.

5. Start jails:

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr start jail1
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr start --all
   ```

6. Restart jails:

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr restart jail1
   /usr/sbin/jailmgr restart --all
   ```

## Notes

- `jailmgr start` uses `jailctl create` followed by `jailctl supervise`.
- By default, bootstrap derives the set URL from `RELEASE` and `MACHINE_ARCH` as:
  `https://cdn.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/NetBSD-${RELEASE}/${MACHINE_ARCH}/binary/sets`
  (override with `NETBSD_MIRROR`).
- Host keys are generated by each jail's `/etc/rc` if missing.
- During `create`, host `/etc/resolv.conf` is copied to each jail's `data/etc/resolv.conf` when present.
- `/etc/rc.d/jailmgr` is installed as part of the regular base system.

- Local per-jail `/dev` setup is enabled by default (`JAIL_LOCAL_DEV=YES`) and can be tuned with `JAIL_DEV_SIZE` and `JAIL_DEVICES` (default: `"std ptm"`).
